al prompt plus the proposed tokens in one forward pass, computing logits for every position in that sequence. This lets it verify multiple draft tokens at once instead of generating them one by one.\n\nThe key trick is rejection sampling. For each proposed token, the target model compares its own probability for that token with the draft model\u2019s probability. If the target model\u2019s probability is at least as high, the token is accepted. If it is lower, the token is rejected and a new token is sampled from a corrected distribution that favors the target model\u2019s probabilities while preserving the target model\u2019s exact output distribution. After the first rejected token, the remaining draft tokens are discarded. Because every accepted or resampled token is drawn from the target model\u2019s conditional distribution, the final sequence has the same distribution as ordinary autoregressive decoding with the target model alone.\n\nIn practice, speedup depends on how often the draft model\u2019s proposals are accepted, the cost of running the target model on the longer sequence, and the relative latency of the draft model. A high acceptance rate and a cheap draft model produce large gains, while a slow draft model or expensive verification reduces them. The two most common reasons the speedup is smaller than hoped are low draft-target agreement, especially on difficult or diverse prompts, and the overhead of running the draft model plus the target model\u2019s longer verification pass.\"\n\nCount words. Let's count manually.\nSpeculative1", "top2": [[[1596, -0.002059970749542117], [22916, -6.88720703125]], [[1144, -1.7047073924914002e-05], [4087, -11.760696411132812]], [[4087, -0.21496112644672394], [310, -1.6526269912719727]], [[1156, -0.002433337504044175], [303, -6.0881876945495605]], [[579, -0.2664068639278412], [25, -1.7436789274215698]], [[1622, -0.010998791083693504], [3296, -4.774096488952637]], [[25, -0.3284152150154114], [13, -1.290151834487915]],